  {
   "id": "7nhxJgMsBAAJ",
   "from": "Matt Gould",
   "author": "a0c39d845d86dc4f",
   "date": "2017-08-12T23:50:45Z",
   "subject": "Effect of Occasion Length on Estimated Denstiy",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><font color=\"#000000\">Hi Everyone,</font></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-size:12.8px\">I&#39;m working with some camera trap data for mountain lions, and I was curious about how estimated density may be affected by sampling occasion length? For all passive detectors, we are continuously sampling a population and during analysis, we break this sampling into discrete time periods. Usually, we want to bin time into the shortest possible interval while maintaining independence between occasions. Yet, creating longer occasions may be necessary with sparse data to </span>ensure<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-size:12.8px\"> capture probabilities aren&#39;t so low that parameter estimation becomes an issue. </span><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><font color=\"#000000\">Given that,<span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"> I was curious how 10 19-day occasions might affect an estimate compared to 14 14-day or 26 7-day occasions? Since camera traps collect count information, I was thinking there wouldn&#39;t be too much of an effect given you don&#39;t lose any capture information. Instead, the biggest concern would be if detection probability fluctuated quite frequently and a shorter occasion length would allow you to more accurately represent that variation. </span></font></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><font color=\"#000000\">I&#39;ve read Borchers et al.&#39;s (2014) paper on continuous-time models, and their findings seem to suggest shorter occasions would be better in order to approximate continuous time. Yet, they also compared their CT model to binary data, which would contain less capture information than count data.</font></span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><font color=\"#000000\">Is my reasoning flawed? Any thoughts or suggestions on the matter would be greatly appreciated. Thanks for your time and take care.</font></span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><font color=\"#000000\">Best,</font></span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:12.8px;font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><font color=\"#000000\">Matt </font></span></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "OFoKSpp-BAAJ",
   "from": "Murray Efford",
